.TH mkdir "" "Command"
.SH NAME
mkdir \- make a new directory
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBmkdir \fIdirectory ...\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
mkdir -- make a new directory
Usage:	mkdir directory ...
.HE
.B mkdir
creates each named
.IR directory .
For each
.IR directory ,
it verifies that the parent directory exists and that the caller has
write and search permission on it, then creates
.I directory
itself together with the two entries
.B .
and
.B ..
that every directory must contain:
.B .
links to
.I directory
itself, and
.B ..
links to its parent.
.PP
Because only the superuser may create the underlying directory node,
.B mkdir
is normally installed set-user-id to root; the new directory is then
given to the invoking user with
.BR chown() ,
so that it is owned by the user who ran
.B mkdir
rather than by root.
.PP
.B mkdir
catches the
.B SIGINT
and
.B SIGHUP
signals while it works, so that an interrupt cannot leave behind a
half-formed directory that lacks its
.B .
or
.B ..
entry.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B mkdir
returns a nonzero exit status if any
.I directory
could not be created, after printing one of the following complaints
to the standard error:
.IP "cannot get child dir name %s"
The last component of
.I directory
could not be extracted from the pathname.
.IP "%s not allowed"
The last component of
.I directory
is
.B .
or
.BR .. ,
which cannot be created as a new directory.
.IP "parent dir %s does not exist"
The directory that would contain
.I directory
does not exist.
.IP "no permission to mkdir in %s"
The caller lacks write and search permission on the parent directory.
.IP "%s already exists"
.I directory
(or a file of that name) is already present.
.IP "not the super-user"
The underlying directory node could not be created because
.B mkdir
is not running with superuser privilege.
.IP "link to %s failed"
The
.B .
or
.B ..
entry could not be linked into the new directory; in this case
.B mkdir
removes the partially made directory.
.IP "cannot make %s"
The underlying directory node could not be created, for a reason
other than a missing parent or a lack of privilege.
.IP "out of memory"
.B mkdir
could not allocate working storage for a pathname.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR mknod ,
.B rmdir
.SH NOTES
.B mkdir
will not create a directory named
.B .
or
.BR .. .
